<ServiceLayout
  slug="garage-conversions"
  metaTitle="Garage Conversions San Diego | ADU & Bonus Room — Reliable Builders"
  metaDescription="Licensed San Diego garage conversions — turn your garage into a legal ADU, JADU, or finished bonus room. Honest feasibility checks, one accountable crew. CSLB #1139785."
  eyebrow="Garage Conversions · San Diego"
  headline="Turn your garage into real living space."
  subhead="A legal ADU, junior ADU, or finished bonus room — built inside the structure you already have, by one accountable crew that tells you straight what’s possible."
  heroImage="/images/work-adu-2.jpg"
  priceRange="From $90K"
  includes={includes}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <h2>The space you need might already be sitting in your driveway.</h2>
  <p>
    Most homeowners assume adding a living space means a big, expensive detached build in the backyard.
    But if you have a garage, you already own the hardest and most expensive parts of a new structure:
    the foundation, the walls, and the roof. A garage conversion turns that existing shell into
    finished, livable square footage — which is why it’s usually the <em>faster and more affordable</em>
    path to an extra unit.
  </p>
  <p>
    That matters for the kind of project a lot of San Diego families are facing right now: a spot for a
    grown child who can’t afford their own place yet, a safe setup for an aging parent, a rental that
    helps cover the mortgage, or a quiet home office that isn’t the corner of a bedroom. A converted
    garage can do any of those.
  </p>
  <h3>Start with honesty, not demolition</h3>
  <p>
    Here’s where we’re different from the crews that will tell you anything to get started. Before we
    touch your garage, we do a feasibility check. Can your lot legally support an ADU or junior ADU?
    What do the setbacks, utilities, and permits actually require? We’d rather spend an afternoon
    answering those questions honestly than have you three weeks into a build discovering it won’t pass.
  </p>
  <p>
    California’s statewide ADU laws have genuinely made these projects easier than they were even a few
    years ago — the state has pushed hard to let homeowners add units. But the rules still come down to
    your specific property. We’ll read your lot, tell you what’s allowed, and lay out the honest path
    before you spend a dollar on construction.
  </p>
  <h3>What “finished” really means</h3>
  <p>
    A garage was built to park a car and store boxes — not to be comfortable. So the real work of a
    conversion is everything you can’t see from the driveway. We insulate the walls, ceiling, and the
    cold edges of the slab. We frame and drywall so the proportions feel like a room instead of a
    bay. We add heating and cooling so it’s livable in August and January alike. Done right, nobody
    walking in should be able to tell it used to be a garage.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you’re building a full ADU, that also means a small kitchen and a full bathroom. The plumbing is
    the part homeowners underestimate most — running water and drain lines to a space that never had
    them is real work. We rough it in properly and handle the tile and countertop in-house, so the
    kitchenette and bath are the parts you’re proudest of, not the parts you quietly apologize for.
  </p>
  <h3>Utilities are where shortcuts show up later</h3>
  <p>
    A converted garage needs to run on its own: power, water, often its own sub-panel, and sometimes a
    separate address for a rental. This is the invisible backbone, and it’s exactly where cut-rate crews
    cut corners — an undersized electrical feed, a drain that barely slopes, a water heater crammed where
    it shouldn’t be. We size it for how the space will actually be used, permit it where required, and do
    it once so you’re not opening walls back up in two years.
  </p>
  <h3>What your investment gets you</h3>
  <p>
    A full garage-to-ADU conversion typically starts around <strong>$90K</strong>, and the number moves
    based on how much new plumbing, electrical, and finish work the space needs. A simpler bonus-room
    finish — insulation, drywall, flooring, and climate control, without a full kitchen and bath — can
    land well below that. On the first visit we’ll look at your garage, talk through how you want to use
    it, and give you an honest range for your exact project. No pressure, no “sign today” nonsense — just
    a straight answer about what it takes and what it costs.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
